{"key":"u23-006-v3","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"A","final_answer":"y = 1/2 − (x − 2)/4","working":"Let f(x) = 1/x. At x = 2, the y-coordinate is f(2) = 1/2, so the point of tangency is (2, 1/2). The derivative is f'(x) = -1/x^2. The slope of the tangent line at x = 2 is m = f'(2) = -1/(2^2) = -1/4. Using the point-slope form of a linear equation, y - y_0 = m(x - x_0), we have y - 1/2 = -1/4(x - 2), which gives y = 1/2 - (x - 2)/4. This corresponds to choice A.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":414,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":414,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":443,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":209,"reasoningTokens":234},"totalTokens":857,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":234,"promptTokenCount":414,"candidatesTokenCount":209,"totalTokenCount":857,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":414}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":209}]},"reasoningTokens":234,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":3975,"keyed_label":"A"}
