{"key":"u23-028-v3","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"C","final_answer":"h is not differentiable at x = 1 because h is not continuous at x = 1.","working":"We examine the continuity of h at x = 1. The left-hand limit is lim(x->1-) x^2 = 1. The right-hand limit is lim(x->1+) (x + 2) = 3. Since the one-sided limits are not equal, lim(x->1) h(x) does not exist, meaning h is not continuous at x = 1. Because differentiability requires continuity, h cannot be differentiable at x = 1. Therefore, statement C is true.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":436,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":436,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":524,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":164,"reasoningTokens":360},"totalTokens":960,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":360,"promptTokenCount":436,"candidatesTokenCount":164,"totalTokenCount":960,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":436}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":164}]},"reasoningTokens":360,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":4626,"keyed_label":"C"}
